        "<b>Chronic pelvic pain</b> is persistent pain perceived in pelvic structures, usually for six months or longer (some definitions use three months), often with bladder, bowel, sexual, or gynecologic symptoms and with cognitive, behavioral, and emotional consequences. It includes conditions such as bladder pain syndrome (interstitial cystitis), endometriosis-related pain, vulvodynia, provoked vestibulodynia, coccydynia, pudendal neuralgia, and chronic prostatitis/chronic pelvic pain syndrome in men. Many patients see several providers before anyone examines their musculoskeletal system.",
        "Two mechanisms dominate PT thinking. First, the <b>overactive pelvic floor</b>: muscles that hold high tone and cannot fully relax, often with trigger points, contribute to pain, painful intercourse (dyspareunia), urinary hesitancy, and constipation. Second, <b>central sensitization</b>: persistent nociceptive input and stress can increase nervous system sensitivity so pain is amplified and spreads beyond the original tissue. These patients often have overlapping conditions such as irritable bowel syndrome, migraine, and fibromyalgia, and high rates of anxiety, depression, or past trauma. A trauma-informed approach, which gives the patient control, explains every step, and obtains ongoing consent, is essential.",
        "PT management is biopsychosocial. <b>Pain neuroscience education</b> explains that pain is a protective output of the nervous system, not a direct measure of tissue damage, which lowers threat. <b>Downtraining</b> teaches pelvic floor relaxation through diaphragmatic breathing, body scanning, positioning, and biofeedback. Manual therapy to the hips, abdomen, thoracolumbar region, and (by trained clinicians) internal trigger points; graded exercise; bowel and bladder habits; and graded exposure to feared activities, including return to sexual activity, round out care. For a weak pelvic floor, strengthening helps; for an overactive one, strengthening first can make pain worse. Screen for red flags, such as unexplained bleeding, fever, weight loss, or new neurologic deficits, and coordinate with gynecology, urology, gastroenterology, and mental health providers.",
        "<b>Men's pelvic health</b> is under-recognized. Chronic prostatitis/chronic pelvic pain syndrome (CP/CPPS) presents as pain in the perineum, testicles, penis tip, suprapubic region, or with ejaculation, usually without infection. Many men have pelvic floor overactivity and tenderness, and the same downtraining and pain science approach applies. Assessment tools such as the NIH-Chronic Prostatitis Symptom Index track outcomes.",
        "<b>Post-prostatectomy incontinence</b> follows radical prostatectomy for prostate cancer. Removing the prostate removes part of the urethral closure mechanism, so the external urethral sphincter and pelvic floor must take over. Most men have stress incontinence initially, and most improve substantially over the first year, but some remain incontinent. PFMT, ideally started before surgery and resumed after catheter removal, is associated with faster recovery of continence in many studies. Teach men to contract by imagining stopping urine flow or lifting the penis and drawing the testicles up, without gluteal or abdominal substitution. Progress from lying to standing to functional tasks, add the knack before coughing and lifting, and address urgency with bladder training if present. Erectile function may also be affected, and pelvic floor training may help some men.",
        "<b>Worked example.</b> A 34-year-old man has 9 months of perineal pain worse with prolonged sitting and after ejaculation, urinary hesitancy, and negative urine cultures. He is anxious and fears cancer. External exam shows tender adductors and ischial region, and he cannot relax his pelvic floor after a contraction. You suspect CP/CPPS with pelvic floor overactivity. Your plan: explain pain mechanisms in plain terms, teach diaphragmatic breathing with pelvic floor relaxation, modify sitting (cushion, breaks), add hip mobility and aerobic exercise, and refer to a pelvic health specialist for internal assessment. You avoid Kegel strengthening at this stage."
